blurb = "Depicts a ship in harbor at Shimonoseki in Nagato Province (present-day Yamaguchi prefecture) on the west tip of Japan's main island.

The ship appears to be a benzaisen, a large coastal cargo vessel. Such ships were often called sengokubune (\"thousand-koku ship\"), referring to their ability to carry a thousand koku worth of cargo (about 150 tons).

The koku is a unit of volume used to measure rice, equivalent to about 180 liters, or 150kg of rice. It was considered to be enough rice to feed one person for a year, and as such by the 1660s it became a standard unit for salaries and taxation. Feudal domains were often measured in terms of how many koku of rice they produced (\"kokudaka\"), and to be a daimyō (feudal lord) required one's domain to produce at least 10,000 koku of rice."
metadata/_custom_type_script = "uid://6kpsqyb2pk7a"
